+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -uo pipefail
+
+if [[ "$#" -lt 2 ]]; then
+  echo "usage: run_logged.sh LOG COMMAND [ARG ...]" >&2
+  exit 64
+fi
+
+log=$1
+shift
+mkdir -p "$(dirname "$log")"
+
+{
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+} >"$log"
+
+set +e
+"$@" > >(tee -a "$log") 2> >(tee -a "$log" >&2)
+status=$?
+set -e
+printf '\nEXIT_STATUS: %d\n' "$status" | tee -a "$log"
+exit "$status"

codex
The submitted translation is byte-identical when regenerated with the trusted translator, and all four documented examples agree. The broader differential found a material unrestricted-domain divergence: for `(9007199254740993, 9007199254740992.0)`, the trusted canonical returns `None` after float conversion, while the submitted program returns the integer. Across 1,094 intended-domain cases, 139 diverged (including large-integer precision and overflow boundaries). I’m continuing because the proof itself must still be reconstructed and statically audited independently.
